1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,056
Select Simulate at the top of Circuit Studio.

2
00:00:03,306 --> 00:00:08,772
The bench combines a function generator, oscilloscope, Bode plot, transfer curve, DC values,

3
00:00:08,772 --> 00:00:10,454
and a data log.

4
00:00:10,704 --> 00:00:13,768
We will use the built-in RC low-pass experiment.

5
00:00:14,018 --> 00:00:18,647
Its resistor is one kilo-ohm and its capacitor is one microfarad.

6
00:00:18,897 --> 00:00:23,268
These values give a corner frequency near one hundred fifty-nine hertz.

7
00:00:25,068 --> 00:00:28,055
In the function generator, choose the source to drive.

8
00:00:28,305 --> 00:00:33,647
Set Sine, one volt peak amplitude, zero DC offset, and one hundred hertz.

9
00:00:33,897 --> 00:00:39,443
Peak amplitude is not peak-to-peak amplitude: this input spans two volts peak to peak.

10
00:00:39,693 --> 00:00:44,653
Live reruns after changes; Run performs a new simulation explicitly.

11
00:00:46,453 --> 00:00:50,110
Use the channel menus or Pick buttons to select the probes.

12
00:00:50,360 --> 00:00:53,891
Put channel one at V in, the node before the resistor.

13
00:00:54,141 --> 00:00:57,991
Put channel two at V out, where the resistor and capacitor meet.

14
00:00:58,241 --> 00:01:01,463
These node voltages are referenced to circuit ground.

15
00:01:01,713 --> 00:01:07,792
Keeping channel one as input and channel two as output makes the gain interpretation clear.

16
00:01:09,592 --> 00:01:13,538
The oscilloscope displays input in yellow and output in green.

17
00:01:13,788 --> 00:01:17,905
Set both voltage scales to the same value when comparing their heights.

18
00:01:18,155 --> 00:01:21,388
Auto scaling can make a smaller signal look taller.

19
00:01:21,638 --> 00:01:27,817
At one hundred hertz, the output is about zero point eight five times the input and lags it

20
00:01:27,817 --> 00:01:29,190
by about thirty-two degrees.

21
00:01:30,990 --> 00:01:35,916
Drag the separator above the bench upward when you need more room for plots and readings.

22
00:01:36,166 --> 00:01:39,358
The Pop out button opens the bench in a separate window.

23
00:01:39,608 --> 00:01:44,690
Settle controls how many cycles are allowed before measurement, and Show controls the

24
00:01:44,690 --> 00:01:45,472
displayed cycles.

25
00:01:45,722 --> 00:01:50,307
Use enough settling time, especially for circuits with long time constants.

26
00:01:52,107 --> 00:01:56,138
Choose Bode plot to examine the small-signal frequency response.

27
00:01:56,388 --> 00:02:02,493
With channel one on input and channel two on output, the plotted ratio is output divided by

28
00:02:02,493 --> 00:02:02,852
input.

29
00:02:03,102 --> 00:02:08,320
This low-pass circuit is close to unity at low frequency, then attenuates higher

30
00:02:08,320 --> 00:02:08,722
frequencies.

31
00:02:08,972 --> 00:02:14,347
The minus three decibel corner is near one hundred fifty-nine hertz, consistent with one

32
00:02:14,347 --> 00:02:16,267
over two pi R C.

33
00:02:18,067 --> 00:02:20,544
Transfer curve is a different experiment.

34
00:02:20,794 --> 00:02:25,158
It sweeps the generator’s DC level and plots output against input.

35
00:02:25,408 --> 00:02:29,226
Capacitors are open circuits in this DC calculation.

36
00:02:29,476 --> 00:02:34,436
Our unloaded RC output therefore follows the input with a slope near one.

37
00:02:34,686 --> 00:02:40,464
That does not contradict the high-frequency attenuation in the Bode plot: the two analyses

38
00:02:40,464 --> 00:02:41,702
ask different questions.

39
00:02:43,502 --> 00:02:47,404
Return to the oscilloscope and raise frequency to one kilohertz.

40
00:02:47,654 --> 00:02:49,566
Keep the same channel scales.

41
00:02:49,816 --> 00:02:56,218
The output is now much smaller, about zero point one six times the input, with a lag near

42
00:02:56,218 --> 00:02:56,930
eighty-one degrees.

43
00:02:57,180 --> 00:03:00,655
This is the behavior predicted by the low-pass response.

44
00:03:00,905 --> 00:03:05,002
Change one setting at a time so the comparison remains meaningful.

45
00:03:06,802 --> 00:03:10,790
Select Record after a useful measurement, then open Data log.

46
00:03:11,040 --> 00:03:15,348
Here we have recorded the one hundred hertz case and the one kilohertz case.

47
00:03:15,598 --> 00:03:20,858
Compare the generator settings and output amplitudes, then use the instrument readouts to

48
00:03:20,858 --> 00:03:22,476
inspect gain and phase.

49
00:03:22,726 --> 00:03:28,408
A saved measurement table makes your explanation more convincing than a screenshot of a

50
00:03:28,408 --> 00:03:30,437
waveform with no setup information.

51
00:03:32,237 --> 00:03:37,827
Use the available export controls to keep plots or measurement data for your lab report.

52
00:03:38,077 --> 00:03:43,909
Results depend on the device models and assumptions, so inspect those when working with

53
00:03:43,909 --> 00:03:45,159
transistors or amplifiers.

54
00:03:45,409 --> 00:03:50,710
Finish by stating what you changed, what you measured, and why the circuit behaved that way.

55
00:03:50,960 --> 00:03:54,789
Save the editable project so you can reproduce the experiment.
